  "enhanced_information": "The patient's symptoms of intermittent episodes of dizziness characterized by a sensation of the room spinning, along with intermittent ringing and fullness in the right ear, and hearing better in the left ear, suggest vertigo associated with an inner ear problem. The Weber test lateralizing to the left ear indicates right ear hearing loss. These symptoms align with Meniere's disease, which involves reduced resorption of endolymph. The evidence of horizontal nystagmus to the right further supports an inner ear disorder rather than a central neurological issue. Other options such as cerebellopontine angle tumor, occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery, and obstruction of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery are less likely due to the absence of additional neurological signs or more severe symptoms typically associated with these conditions. The option of demyelinating plaques is also unlikely given the lack of other typical neurological symptoms such as vision changes or limb weakness. Therefore, reduced resorption of endolymph consistent with Meniere's disease is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms."
